  "notes": "Standard Tamil (Tamil Nadu, India + Sri Lanka). Dravidian language, not related to Indo-Aryan. Key features: no aspirated stop phonemes (aspiration is allophonic); voiced/voiceless distinction positional (not phonemic for native words); two distinct non-retroflex rhotic letters — ⟨ர⟩ is the alveolar tap [ɾ] and never geminates, ⟨ற⟩ the trill [r] whose orthographic geminate ⟨ற்ற⟩ is long (Keane 2004: 113; Schiffman 1999: 7); unique retroflex approximant ⟨ழ⟩ = [ɻ]; retroflex lateral ⟨ள⟩ = [ɭ]; alveolar ⟨ன⟩ vs dental ⟨ந⟩. Strong diglossia: literary (centamiḻ) vs spoken (kōccai tamiḻ). Oldest attested Dravidian language (inscriptions from 3rd c. BCE). Wave audit (2026): the wikipron/ta row (n=10093, PER 0.3757) was checked against the hypothesis that its residual error is unmodelled stop-voicing allophony; it is not. A full confusion-matrix decomposition (hypothesis vs best-matching gold reference, all 10093 words) shows the dominant edits are vowel-quality notation, not consonant voicing: a→ɐ 11999, i→ɪ 7164, u→ʊ 5551, a→ɑ 3356 (≈28,070 of ≈28,400 total edits), against stop-voicing confusions of p↔b 112, k↔ɡ 99 and t↔d 77 (≈288 edits, about 1%). The gold writes narrow allophonic vowel symbols even in non-reduced syllables, while Keane (2004) treats /i a u/ as the phonemic inventory; reproducing the gold's narrow transcription blanket would misdescribe the language rather than model a positional process, so this is a transcription-convention ceiling the spec cannot close. The 102 TA_GEM*/TA_NOVOICE_*/TA_VOICE_* rules already correctly model the voiceless-initial/geminate vs voiced-intervocalic/post-nasal alternation (Keane 2004; Krishnamurti 2003); this wave added no new voicing rule. The residual voicing confusions split into two classes that a grapheme-to-phoneme rule spec cannot reach: loanwords that keep source-language voicing against the native rule, e.g. கஜானா 'khazana' (hyp kadʒaːnaː, word-initial voiceless per rule, vs gold ɡɐdʑɑːnɑː) and ரம்சான் 'Ramzan' (hyp voices the post-nasal stop per rule, vs gold's unvoiced ɾɐmtɕɑːn) — fixable only with a loanword lexicon; and compound-boundary sandhi, where a second compound member's initial stop surfaces voiceless despite sitting in a surface-intervocalic position, e.g. கருதுகோள் (hyp ...duɡoːɭ rule-correct intervocalic voicing, vs gold ...dukoːɭ) — fixable only with a compound segmenter the spec does not have. Both are named as scope for a future wave, not defects in the current rules. Checked and clean: gold_composition.py's trivial/real split gives trivial_share 0.0257 (270/10492) with real-word PER 0.3767 against the 0.3757 headline — this row is not flattered by alphabet-chart entries, unusually tight agreement for a row this size; the shipped tokenizer emits only two UNKNOWN characters over the whole gold, the Tamil OM sign ௐ and the AU length mark ௗ, one word each; the Grantha consonants (ஶ ஜ ஷ ஸ ஹ க்ஷ) are already mapped in the grapheme table; and no ZWJ/ZWNJ leakage was found. 355 of 10095 distinct spellings (3.5%) carry two or more gold transcriptions, which is evidence the orthography underdetermines the pronunciation, not a score floor — the harness scores each word against the best of its references, so a homograph makes a word easier, not unreachable.",
