      "pages": "111-116",
      "notes": "The stop series has no voicing contrast: plosives are voiceless word-initially and when geminated, voiced intervocalically and after a nasal. Source for TA_GEM*, TA_NOVOICE_* and TA_VOICE_*. Page 113 ('Liquids') is the source for the two rhotic letters: ⟨ர⟩ is listed under Tap in the consonant chart and glossed கரி [kaɾi] 'charcoal', and Balasubramanian (1982a) is cited there as arguing that ⟨ர⟩ 'always corresponds phonetically to a tap, whilst [ɾ] and [r] are both possible realizations of' ⟨ற⟩; the same page states that rhotic liquids 'are the only category of sounds in Tamil that do not undergo gemination'. Page 112 is the source for the intervocalic realization of ⟨ச⟩ as [s] ('The most common realization of a single intervocalic segment is the alveolar fricative [s]').",
