      "notes": "Voicing guard. Tamil has NO phonemic voicing contrast in stops: voicing is positional (Keane 2004: 111-116; Keane 2004: 112, 'Obstruents': 'voicing is not contrastive in the native Dravidian vocabulary ... In word-initial position voiceless plosives are found ... Voiced obstruents occur word-internally after nasal segments'). A stop is VOICELESS word-initially and when it follows another vowel-less obstruent or liquid (i.e. inside a cluster that is not nasal+stop): பார்ப்பு, கற்க. This identity rewrite fires first in exactly that context and — first match wins — blocks TA_VOICE_*. Both rhotic letters are in the list — the tap ⟨ர⟩ [ɾ] and the trill ⟨ற⟩ [r] behave alike as the first member of a cluster: சார்பு [tɕaːɾpu], கற்க [karka]. Nasals are deliberately absent from the list: a stop AFTER a nasal is voiced (TA_VOICE_*), தங்கம் [t̪aŋɡam]."
